    61	The granularity research Alex asked for is done as a discussion draft (2026-10-22): `docs/granularity-research.md`,
    62	with the raw reports in `docs/research/2026-10-22-granularity/`. It answers Alex's three-layer diagram's gap 1 ("Data ≠
    63	Intelligence") and proposes, for Alex to decide: five tests a candidate view must pass (contested; one settling print;
    64	position-changing; not a statement line restated; not an angle), a budget of 2–4 open company views per object, and
    65	whether the market's expected rung is kept next to the state (four options; lean: layer 2 fetches it from a data
    66	vendor and maps it onto the ladder, nothing added to layer 1; the state is the system's own consensus, the model's
    67	judgement from evidence, D51). Joyce's challenges and the answers so far are logged one by
    68	one in `docs/joyce-challenge.md` (13 entries, incl. the 2026-10-22 call). Claude's synthesis of all of it is
    69	`docs/killer-experience.md`: a position is a bundle of unwritten bets; the user says which bets and where they fold;
    70	Hivemind is the settlement layer (not research, screener or advisor); three of the five gap-2 gaps become mandatory
    71	(events, timeliness, code-checked settlement) and two optional (market expectation, probability per rung). A
    72	direction, not a decision. The product-level experience PRD (`docs/experience-prd.md`, rendered as
    73	`docs/experience-prd.html`, ninth version, 2026-10-23) holds the one-line vision (trading and investing are
    74	becoming something agents do end to end; today's agents lack the middle step that turns a piece of information into
    75	a judgement that can be stated, checked and settled; this product is that step: it takes anything the user has, a
    76	headline, someone's post, a feeling, works out what it bets on, where the evidence stands and when the answer comes,
